          "case_name": "Suter v. Artist M.",
          "citation": "503 U.S. 347 (1992)",
          "court": "Supreme Court of the United States",
          "year": 1992,
          "date_decided": "1992-03-25",
          "holding": "The Supreme Court held that the Adoption Assistance and Child Welfare Act's reasonable efforts requirement creates no privately enforceable right, so beneficiaries cannot sue to enforce it under the Act itself or under 42 U.S.C. 1983.",
          "relevance": "Citizen Review Panels and similar CAPTA structures are advisory precisely because the underlying federal conditions are not privately enforceable.",
          "quote": "We hold that the Act does not create an enforceable right on behalf of respondents.",
          "url": "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/112712/suter-v-artist-m/",
          "courtlistener_cluster_id": 112712
        },
        {
          "case_name": "Mercer v. South Dakota Attorney General Office",
          "citation": "2015 S.D. 31, 864 N.W.2d 299",
          "court": "South Dakota Supreme Court",
          "year": 2015,
          "date_decided": "2015-05-13",
          "holding": "The court affirmed denial of a public records request for a death investigation file, holding SDCL 1-27-1.5(5) and SDCL 23-5-11 expressly exempt law enforcement investigative reports and confidential criminal justice information, and that no right to redaction or partial release exists.",
          "relevance": "Any citizen review body seeking underlying investigative records in South Dakota must reckon with this exemption.",
          "quote": "The records developed or received by the agencies charged with the duty to investigate Benda's death constitute confidential criminal justice information as defined by statute.",
          "url": "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/2800709/mercer-v-south-dakota-attorney-general-office/",
          "courtlistener_cluster_id": 2800709
        },
        {
          "case_name": "Matter of Implicated Individual",
          "citation": "2023 S.D. 16, 989 N.W.2d 517",
          "court": "South Dakota Supreme Court",
          "year": 2023,
          "date_decided": "2023-04-05",
          "holding": "The court affirmed the order unsealing search warrant affidavits after the investigation closed, holding no statute entitles the implicated individual to inspect the affidavits or participate in redaction before unsealing, and the circuit court did not abuse its discretion in denying that request.",
          "relevance": "It supplies the mechanism by which an outside body can obtain sealed investigative court records once the investigation terminates.",
          "quote": "There is nothing in SDCL 23A-35-4.1 or SDCL 15-15A-13 that mandates the circuit court to allow such an inspection.",
          "url": "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/9385298/matter-of-implicated-individual/",
          "courtlistener_cluster_id": 9389822
        }
      ],
